   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 217 of the laws of 2015, amending the education law\nrelating to certified school psychologists and special education services and\nprograms for preschool children with handicapping conditions, in relation to\nthe effectiveness thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S7641",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7641-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §3, Chap 217 of 2015",
    "lawSection": "Education",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7641\n\nSPONSOR: LAVALLE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend chapter 217 of the laws of 2015, amending the education\nlaw relating to certified school psychologists and special education\nservices and programs for preschool children with handicapping condi-\ntions, in relation to the effectiveness thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo extend the provisions of Chapter 217 of the Laws of 2015.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Extend the provision of chapter 217 of the laws of 2015 to\nJune 30, 2020.\n\nSection 2. Sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nWhen this legislation was originally enacted, it amended paragraph (d)\nsubdivision 6 of section 4410 of the education law to allow multidisi-\nplinary evaluation programs approved pursuant to section 4410 of the\neducation law to employ a certified school psychologist on a full or\npart time salary basis which may include an hourly, weekly or monthly\nbasis or on a fee for evaluation services basis provided that the school\npsychologist is employed by and under the dominion and control of a\nprogram that has been approved pursuant to paragraph b of subdivision 9\nof section 4410 or subdivision 9-a of section 4410 to conduct a multi-\ndisciplinary evaluation of a preschool child having or suspected of\nhaving a disability.\n\nThe enacted legislation also amended subdivision 1 of section 7605 of\nthe education law to exempt from licensure until June 30, 2016 certified\nschool psychologists employed on a full time or part time salary basis\nwhich may include an hourly, weekly or monthly basis or on a fee for\nevaluation services basis provided that the certified school psychol-\nogist is employed by and under the dominion and control of a program\nthat has been approved in accordance with title two-A of article twenty\nfive of the public health law to deliver early intervention programs for\nthe purpose of conduction multidisciplinary evaluations, service where\npreschool special education program or early interventions programs\nservices are authorized.\n\nSection 4410 of the education law allows approved center based programs\nto employ a certified school psychologist in a preschool setting.\nRecently, the State Education Department issued a special education\nfield advisory and learned that multidisciplinary evaluation programs\nare also using certified school psychologists to conduct evaluations of\npreschool students with disabilities even though they are not permitted\nunder law to do so. In speaking with the field, shortages of licensed\npsychologists have made it difficult for these multidisciplinary evalu-\nation programs to ensure timely psychological evaluations of preschool\nstudents with disabilities as is required by law. The federal Individ-\nuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) requires that initial evalu-\nations of preschool children with disabilities be conducted within 60\ndays of parental consent for the evaluation. In addition, state law\nrequires that approved multidisciplinary evaluation programs selected by\nthe parent, conduct such evaluations. It is important that these evalu-\nations continue to occur in a timely manner. This legislation would\ntemporarily allow multidisciplinary evaluation programs to continue to\nemploy a certified school psychologist to conduct these evaluations\nwhile the issue is further investigated. In addition, the bill makes\nclear that nothing in the legislation should be interpreted to authorize\na certified school psychologist or group of such psychologists to inde-\npendently practice or form an independent group.\n\nThe previous enacted legislation extended for two years and will expire\non June 30, 2018. This legislation provides for another two-year exten-\nsion to 2020.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTBD\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall be deemed to have been\nin full force and effect on and after July 1, 2014, provided, however,\nthat the provisions of this act shall expire and be deemed repealed June\n30, 2020.\n",
